在那里，一开始很困难，你冷静了一点，然后结束时变得困难，我们只是经历了一堆小循环，你知道，哦，嘿，这就是我们所处的位置，我已经习惯了。我已经把我的情况以一种舒适的方式安顿下来了，然后经历了一个星期的“哦，不，这太糟糕了，我无法处理这个”。而且，你知道，我的孩子们什么时候才能回去上学？或者我的工作什么时候，你知道，再次成为面对面的？

41
00:23:35.000 --> 00:24:00.000
Or when will I get my job back? And so I think it's unfortunately hard to find real relief for any length of time when the situation is constantly evolving and there's no clear end in sight. So what are you working on now? And what are the next big questions that you want to answer? Yeah, I've been thinking a lot about that. You know, I've been studying this very focused question about like, what is this thing that is waiting? and how do we make it easier for about 10 years now?
或者我什么时候能拿回我的工作？所以我认为不幸的是，当情况不断演变且看不到明确的尽头时，很难找到任何长时间的真正缓解。那么你现在在做什么？你想回答的下一个重大问题是什么？是的，我一直在思考很多。你知道，我已经研究了大约 10 年这个非常集中的问题，比如，什么是等待？我们如何让它变得更容易？

42
00:24:00.000 --> 00:24:27.000
And, you know, it's felt like a big question at times. But I think after about a decade, we've answered a lot of the questions that I kind of had at the beginning. There's more to do. But I think we know a lot now about waiting that we didn't know a decade ago. And so I've started to think about where to next. And there's really three big areas that my lab is focusing on now. One is flow, which we've already talked about. And it turns out that the world of flow research is huge and wild and wacky.
而且，你知道，有时感觉这是一个大问题。但我认为大约十年后，我回答了很多一开始就有的问题。还有更多的事情要做。但我认为我们现在对等待的了解比十年前要多。所以我开始考虑下一步去哪里。我的实验室现在主要关注三个大领域。一个是心流，我们已经讨论过了。事实证明，心流研究的世界是巨大、狂野而古怪的。

43
00:24:27.000 --> 00:24:48.000
That's very interdisciplinary, I guess. It's kind of a nice word for it, which means people from lots of different fields, often not even scientific fields, are interested in it. But that means that it's a mess. I mean, honestly, the research is kind of a mess. And it's really hard to know what we know about flow. And it is this very kind of powerful, compelling experience that, you know, we all have and I think we seek sometimes.
我想这是一个好听的词，具有很强的跨学科性，这意味着来自许多不同领域（通常甚至不是科学领域）的人都对它感兴趣。但这意味它是一团糟。老实说，研究有点一团糟。很难知道我们对心流了解多少。这是一种非常强大、引人注目的体验，你知道，我们都有，我想有时我们也会去寻找。

44
00:24:48.000 --> 00:25:29.000
But what exactly it is and what it looks like in the brain and the body, how exactly it benefits us and when we really don't have a lot of good evidence on that. So, you know, I became interested in flow because of waiting and because of its fit as a coping strategy during waiting periods. But now I and my graduate students have fallen down the rabbit hole a little bit and are interested in getting a better handle on what that exactly is. So that's one area. I'm also taking a deeper dive into worry and what exactly it is about worry during waiting periods that is so difficult. And that is work I'm doing with a collaborator at another University of California campus at Merced, Jennifer Howell, who's a long-time
但它究竟是什么，它在大脑和身体中是什么样子的，它究竟如何使我们受益，以及什么时候我们确实没有很多好的证据来证明这一点。所以，你知道，我对心流感兴趣是因为等待，因为它适合作为等待期间的应对策略。但现在我和我的研究生们有点陷入兔子洞，有兴趣更好地掌握这究竟是什么。所以这是一个领域。我也在深入研究担忧，以及等待期间的担忧到底是什么如此困难。这是我与加州大学默塞德分校的另一位学者詹妮弗·豪威尔（Jennifer Howell）合作完成的工作，她是我长期的合作伙伴。

45
00:25:29.000 --> 00:26:15.000
collaborator of mine. And we have some money right now to try to figure out, you know, when is it that worry is hopeful to us and not so disruptive to our lives? And when is it that it really does become a problem for our health and well-being? And that turns out to be, as things will tend to be in psychology, a more complicated question than might appear on the surface. And then the third big question is one that's a big, big collaboration that I'm really excited about thinking about patience, which in some ways I've been studying all along, but more as a virtue. And that's an interesting way to kind of think about patience, not just as like waiting calmly, but as something that we can kind of cultivate, that does have connections to philosophy and theology. And so
合作者。我们现在有一些资金来试图弄清楚，你知道，什么时候担忧对我们是有希望的，而不是对我们的生活造成太大的破坏？什么时候它真的成为我们健康和幸福的问题？事实证明，正如心理学中的情况一样，这是一个比表面看起来更复杂的问题。第三个重大问题是一个非常大的合作，我对此感到非常兴奋，它思考的是耐心，某种程度上我一直都在研究它，但更多的是作为一种美德。这是一种思考耐心的有趣方式，不仅仅是平静地等待，而是我们可以培养的东西，它与哲学和神学有联系。所以

46
00:26:15.000 --> 00:26:54.000
a researcher at Baylor University, Sarah Schnichter, is leading this big, big, initiative to bring together researchers from across fields to kind of bring our perspectives on what the heck this thing is called patience, you know, what it looks like, how to get it. And one of, to me, the most interesting questions about patience is, why are we so quick to say we don't have it? So most of us, you know, wouldn't quickly say that we are, you know, cowardly or unkind or ungenerous, but lots of people, myself included, will happily say we're inpatience, which makes it kind of a funny virtue. So those are just some of the things. we're digging into in the coming years.
贝勒大学的研究员萨拉·施尼希特正在领导一个非常大的项目，旨在汇集各领域的研人员，从我们的视角来探讨这到底是个什么东西，也就是耐心，你知道它是什么样子的，如何获得它。对我来说，关于耐心最有趣的问题之一是，为什么我们这么快就说自己没有耐心？所以大多数人，你知道，不会轻易说自己是懦弱的、不友善的或吝啬的，但很多人，包括我自己，会很高兴地承认自己缺乏耐心，这让它成了一种很有趣的美德。这些只是我们未来几年将深入研究的一些问题。

